course: dictionary
lesson: 10753
title: A name with a story
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Uriah is a proper noun. A proper noun is the name of a particular person, place or thing, and it always starts with a capital letter. When you see a capital letter in the middle of a sentence, it tells you the word is a name, not an ordinary word.
explain: You can often work out who Uriah is from the words around him. «David took Bathsheba, another man's wife, and arranged for her husband Uriah to be killed in battle.» Uriah is a man, he was Bathsheba's husband, and he died in battle. The clues show the name is a person, even if you know nothing else.
explain: Sometimes a name becomes a word used to describe people. «A Uriah Heep» means a creepy person who pretends to be humble but is really sly. Words like sly and humble in a sentence tell you the kind of person meant. When a dictionary says a name is a person in a story, it is telling you where the name comes from.
fact: Uriah is a proper noun, so it is a name and begins with a capital letter.
fact: Uriah the Hittite was a loyal soldier in King David's army.
fact: «David took Bathsheba, another man's wife» shows that David did a terrible wrong.
fact: The prophet Nathan made David face his sin, and David then repented.
fact: «A Uriah Heep» means a sly, falsely humble villain, from Charles Dickens's novel David Copperfield.
exercise: What kind of word is Uriah? | A common noun for any soldier | *A proper noun, the name of a particular person | An adjective describing a thing | A verb showing an action
exercise: In the Bible story, what was Uriah's job? | A prophet who spoke for God | A novelist in London | *A loyal soldier in King David's army | A king who ruled Jerusalem
exercise: «A Uriah Heep» describes someone who is... | A loud singer | Fast and cheerful | *Creepy and falsely humble | A brave explorer
test: Which name belongs to Uriah's wife in the story? | Miriam | Esther | Ruth | *Bathsheba
test: Who made David face his sin? | *The prophet Nathan | Charles Dickens | Uriah the Hittite | King Saul
exam: «David took Bathsheba, another man's wife, and arranged for her husband Uriah to be killed in battle.» What does this sentence tell us about David? | He wrote a famous novel | He was a soldier in the army | *He did a terrible wrong | A kind king who helped everyone
